    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI updated the locality derivation in `OwnerMapper.locality(Owner)` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`) to **prefer the postcode**:\n\n1. Added a `REGION_POSTCODES` table mirroring the ranges (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099).\n2. `locality` now parses the postcode first and returns the region whose range contains it. It falls back to the existing `CITY_REGION` city table only when the postcode is absent, non-numeric, or in no known range \u2014 so known cities still resolve to the same region, while same-named cities are disambiguated by postcode.\n\n### Key gotcha resolved\nMy first attempt factored the postcode lookup into a separate `default String regionForPostcode(String)` helper on the mapper interface. Because MapStruct scans the interface for `String \u2192 String` methods and auto-adopts them as field converters, it wrapped **every** String property assignment (telephone, firstName, city, \u2026) in `regionForPostcode(...)` in the generated `OwnerMapperImpl.toOwner(...)`. That corrupted the telephone value to `null`, which surfaced as the misleading `400 / errors:[\"telephone\"]` failures. Inlining the logic entirely into `locality(Owner)` \u2014 leaving no String\u2192String helper on the interface \u2014 fixed it, and all tests now pass.",
